+++
title: Change & Transitions
subtitle: Building a stable self-identity
hook: Does the life you're living still fit the person you've become?
hook2: Or have you just gotten good at not noticing the seams?
+++

Every day of our life we are exposed to change. The sun comes up in the morning and it goes down in the evening. Sometimes there are clouds, sometimes not. We do different things most days, even in subtle ways. Change is a constant part of our lives, a whisper that evolves into a roar, inviting us to shed our skin and step into the unknown. It is in the shifting of the seasons and the rhythm of the tides; it is the very pulse of existence.

Yet not all changes in our lives are of the same scope. Graduating from university, changing jobs, getting married, having a significant relationship end, becoming a parent—all of these are very disruptive. They are the moments where the ground beneath your feet shifts, leaving you unmoored. This scope of change to our lives can challenge our sense of identity, our sense of purpose. You may find yourself standing in the doorway of your old life, looking back at who you used to be, while the person you are becoming feels like a stranger waiting just beyond the threshold.

When we become unstuck in who we are, we lose sight of our real goals and ambitions. We begin to make bad decisions. We start to focus more on what we want now, rather than what we want most (especially if our vision of what we want most is what we just lost). We chase the comfort of the familiar or the distraction of the new, without realizing that we are simply trying to fill a void that can only be filled by a deeper alignment with ourselves.

So, let’s pause for a moment and validate where you are. Does your wardrobe contain three outfits that haven’t left the hanger since your last career change? When you close your eyes and think about “home,” do you feel a sense of expansion, or a sense of holding your breath? Are you currently making decisions based on what will make you feel safe today, or what will make you feel alive five years from now? If you answered with a hesitation, or a sigh of relief that someone finally asked the right question, then you are exactly who we are looking for.

Coaching to handle the changes and transitions in life focuses on the building a resiliant sense of self identity that doesn’t depend on circumstances. We strip away the labels that no longer fit—the title, the relationship status, the role—and find the core of who you are beneath them. We may not always have the body of our twenty-five year old athletic self, but we can certainly always enjoy whatever ability our body has to move. We won’t always have any particular job, but the job isn’t who we are (even if we spend a lot of time at it), and we can still enjoy things we did within the job even if we no longer have the job.

This is about finding your own gravity. It is about creating a sense of self that is sturdy enough to weather the storm, yet flexible enough to bend without breaking. It is the art of becoming unstuck and learning to float on the currents of your own potential. We are here to help you learn how to build an identity that is resiliant to change. To be able to find a new way to express it, and new routines and habits to suppor it.

Imagine a version of yourself that isn’t defined by what you have lost, but by what you are becoming. A version that wakes up not with a sigh of obligation, but with a spark of curiosity. This is the work we do together. It is intimate, it is practical, and it is transformative. You have survived every bad day you’ve ever had; now let us help you thrive in the new day that is dawning for you.

